WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:08.680
REGINA HERZLINGER: So the first step in this framework are the caveats.

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:11.250
Just watch out.

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:15.790
Second step is what am I?

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:20.100
What kind of innovation am I?

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:24.930
So in my many years of looking at this, I&amp;#39;ve

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:31.850
categorized innovations into three types of innovations.

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:36.910
So one type is consumer facing.

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:40.400
It&amp;#39;s audience is the consumer.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:47.310
So drugs that sell in drugstores, they&amp;#39;re consumer facing.

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:53.260
Something like a retail medical clinic, maybe a Minute Clinic where you live,

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:56.050
that&amp;#39;s consumer facing.

00:00:56.050 --> 00:01:03.020
This kind of innovation is saying to the customer I&amp;#39;m quick, I&amp;#39;m convenient,

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:04.370
I&amp;#39;m cheap.

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:11.550
And you can reach me without any intermediary in the way.

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:15.080
Another kind of innovation, very different one,

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:18.610
is technology innovation.

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:24.630
Let&amp;#39;s say somebody has a drug that can ameliorate hepatitis

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:30.540
or Crohn&amp;#39;s disease or MS or AIDS.

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:32.540
That&amp;#39;s technology.

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:36.020
This is a burst of science and the challenge

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:39.990
for this person is how do I commercialize it?

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:47.620
How do I make sure that it gets into the hands of the right people in a way that

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:51.450
still enables me to make a living?

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:56.190
Third kind of innovation, very different from the other two.

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:58.690
I call it an integrator.

00:01:58.690 --> 00:02:02.060
This is a chain of something.

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:06.380
So the most ubiquitous kinds of integrators

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:12.630
are stores like McDonald&amp;#39;s or hotels like the Hilton chain

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:16.790
or department stores like Walmart.

00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:19.770
So what do these guys promise?

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:26.950
They&amp;#39;re not promising quick, convenient like the consumer facing ones.

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:30.940
They certainly don&amp;#39;t offer dazzling technology.

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:36.770
They&amp;#39;re not curing AIDS or hepatitis or congestive heart failure.

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:38.240
So what are they doing?

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:44.980
They&amp;#39;re saying we offer consistent products and services

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:48.130
at a relatively low price.

00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:52.200
And the way we do this is we&amp;#39;re a chain.

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:55.850
So if you&amp;#39;ve got a lot of something, you can

00:02:55.850 --> 00:03:03.150
develop metrics of how the work is done, you can measure how the work is done.

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:06.950
You have a lot of scale in a chain.

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:11.280
The economists call this economies of scale.

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:15.100
In other words, you can buy things more cheaply

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:18.660
because you&amp;#39;re buying millions of whatever you&amp;#39;re buying.

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:24.280
How many hundreds of millions of pounds of potatoes does McDonald&amp;#39;s buy?

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:28.230
Well, certainly it&amp;#39;s going to get a better buy per pound

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:33.910
than you and I might get if we go into our local supermarket.

00:03:33.910 --> 00:03:39.090
So why do we have these categories of these three different kinds

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:41.460
of health care ventures?

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:49.360
Because analyzing their business model requires very different kinds

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:51.470
of elements.

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:58.360
And we&amp;#39;ll go back to this in detail as we go into our cases.

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:02.210
But let me give you a simple example of this.

00:04:02.210 --> 00:04:08.810
Let&amp;#39;s say you&amp;#39;re running a chain of retail medical clinics like Minute

00:04:08.810 --> 00:04:10.400
Clinic.

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:14.496
What kind of CEO do you want for this?

00:04:14.496 --> 00:04:21.880
Do you somebody who has a PhD In biotechnology?

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:24.820
I don&amp;#39;t think so.

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:32.150
Do you want somebody who has a lot of experience

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:36.695
with running chains of manufacturing plants?

00:04:36.695 --> 00:04:39.150
I don&amp;#39;t think so either.

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:47.300
What you want is somebody who has a background in consumer facing ventures.

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:51.840
Somebody who knows how to cater to consumers

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:55.560
with the convenience that they want.

00:04:55.560 --> 00:05:00.770
So one of the reasons to be very clear about the differences

00:05:00.770 --> 00:05:04.740
among the three kinds of ventures is they

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:08.600
require different kinds of management.

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:12.280
The managers of a biotechnology companies

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:15.130
and the managers of a Minute Clinic, they&amp;#39;ve

00:05:15.130 --> 00:05:18.890
got to be very different kinds of people.

00:05:18.890 --> 00:05:22.720
So, you know, lectures, they&amp;#39;re so boring.

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:25.150
And we&amp;#39;re going to get done with this soon.

00:05:25.150 --> 00:05:29.800
We&amp;#39;re going to get to case discussions where you&amp;#39;ll do the talking

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:33.050
and I&amp;#39;ll do the listening.

00:05:33.050 --> 00:05:35.610
It&amp;#39;s much more interesting.

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:38.260
People really remember when they talk.

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:41.970
They don&amp;#39;t remember so much when somebody else talks.

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:46.090
Ever notice in a group, people say, well, what I said was,

00:05:46.090 --> 00:05:48.280
they remember that very vividly.

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:51.170
What you said, not so much.

00:05:51.170 --> 00:05:55.750
So let&amp;#39;s take a case example of a venture

00:05:55.750 --> 00:06:00.350
and let&amp;#39;s try to classify what this venture is.

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:04.970
So let&amp;#39;s take a doctor.

00:06:04.970 --> 00:06:10.100
And he&amp;#39;s kind of a computer geek doctor.

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:15.030
So he uses computers a lot and he has written

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:21.480
a program that can go through the voluminous medical literature

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:26.510
and answer questions that people want answers to.

00:06:26.510 --> 00:06:33.310
Like, my doctor says I should have a pacemaker installed.

00:06:33.310 --> 00:06:35.690
Very invasive procedure.

00:06:35.690 --> 00:06:41.940
But if I don&amp;#39;t have the pacemaker installed, she tells me I might die.

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:47.340
So what is it that the medical literature tells me

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:51.590
about the installation of pacemakers?

00:06:51.590 --> 00:06:54.970
What are the long term consequences?

00:06:54.970 --> 00:06:59.110
After all, I&amp;#39;m going to have this machine sitting inside of me.

00:06:59.110 --> 00:07:01.410
I&amp;#39;m kind of worried about that.

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:04.640
What happens five, six years out?

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:06.530
Does it wear out?

00:07:06.530 --> 00:07:08.980
Does it still keep functioning?

00:07:08.980 --> 00:07:15.030
Does it do funny things, like does it go off erratically?

00:07:15.030 --> 00:07:21.620
So this doctor&amp;#39;s figured out a way of taking the huge medical literature,

00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:25.040
it&amp;#39;s beyond the ken of anybody.

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:29.320
In fact, one of the biggest health insurance

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:34.960
companies in the United States made a deal with IBM

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:38.540
that has a computer called Watson.

00:07:38.540 --> 00:07:44.740
So Watson is legendary for having beat the all time Jeopardy!

00:07:44.740 --> 00:07:51.630
champion because it can so easily analyze mountains and mountains of data

00:07:51.630 --> 00:07:54.570
and come up with the right answer.

00:07:54.570 --> 00:08:00.260
And they&amp;#39;re trying to apply Watson for particular uses.

00:08:00.260 --> 00:08:06.250
This guy&amp;#39;s got a much more user friendly version of Watson.

00:08:06.250 --> 00:08:09.700
He&amp;#39;s tried it with a couple of his colleagues.

00:08:09.700 --> 00:08:11.900
They say it&amp;#39;s great.

00:08:11.900 --> 00:08:18.160
He&amp;#39;s tried it with his friends and family, they say it&amp;#39;s fantastic.

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:20.770
This a typical venture.

00:08:20.770 --> 00:08:25.630
Probably not a good idea to begin with, because he&amp;#39;s

00:08:25.630 --> 00:08:31.250
invested thousands of hours in writing this program without really

00:08:31.250 --> 00:08:37.750
knowing who&amp;#39;s going to use it or how it is that they are going to use it.

00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:40.360
But bless his heart, he&amp;#39;s done it.

00:08:40.360 --> 00:08:44.320
So what kind of venture is this?

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:48.390
Is this consumer facing?

00:08:48.390 --> 00:08:51.180
Is this like Minute Clinic?

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:55.600
Quick, convenient, easy?

00:08:55.600 --> 00:08:58.340
I don&amp;#39;t see it.

00:08:58.340 --> 00:09:05.630
If you think about the kind of CEO you want for a consumer facing venture,

00:09:05.630 --> 00:09:11.020
somebody who comes with a consumer marketing

00:09:11.020 --> 00:09:14.520
background like the people in Procter and Gamble

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:19.050
or some of these legendary consumer marketing companies,

00:09:19.050 --> 00:09:23.360
you want that person to head this venture?

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:24.570
I don&amp;#39;t think so.

00:09:24.570 --> 00:09:29.170
So is this a technology venture?

00:09:29.170 --> 00:09:36.280
Not quite like getting a drug that will ameliorate the AIDS or hepatitis

00:09:36.280 --> 00:09:39.180
or Crohn&amp;#39;s disease.

00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:44.070
Technology ventures, they take your breath away

00:09:44.070 --> 00:09:49.460
because they have such novel solutions using science

00:09:49.460 --> 00:09:52.760
to solving the problems of medicine.

00:09:52.760 --> 00:09:57.520
That&amp;#39;s where the PhDs and the propeller heads come around.

00:09:57.520 --> 00:10:01.260
Even though he&amp;#39;s a doc, I don&amp;#39;t see it that way.

00:10:01.260 --> 00:10:03.450
So we&amp;#39;re left with one category.

00:10:03.450 --> 00:10:09.060
It&amp;#39;s kind of a strange category, is this an integrator venture?

00:10:09.060 --> 00:10:12.440
In other words, does it enable health care

00:10:12.440 --> 00:10:18.530
to be delivered in a cheaper and more consistent way?

00:10:18.530 --> 00:10:20.320
Yeah it does.

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:23.410
Because if you search the medical literature

00:10:23.410 --> 00:10:29.390
and you find answers to questions, you will get medical practice

00:10:29.390 --> 00:10:35.990
that is more uniform and that follows what they call evidence based medicine.

00:10:35.990 --> 00:10:41.660
So rather than some erratic application of medicine,

00:10:41.660 --> 00:10:45.810
you&amp;#39;ll get more standardized ways of doing things.

00:10:45.810 --> 00:10:51.140
And when you get more standardized ways of doing things, you know what happens?

00:10:51.140 --> 00:10:53.070
Errors go down.

00:10:53.070 --> 00:10:59.010
When errors go down, quality goes up and costs go down.

00:10:59.010 --> 00:11:04.700
So this is, believe it or not, this is an integrator venture.

00:11:04.700 --> 00:11:06.940
Who&amp;#39;s going to buy this thing?

00:11:06.940 --> 00:11:09.790
Funny you should ask.

00:11:09.790 --> 00:11:15.120
So in the United States and in many developed countries,

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:19.460
people are recognizing the problem with health care

00:11:19.460 --> 00:11:23.770
delivery is that it&amp;#39;s too fragmented.

00:11:23.770 --> 00:11:27.690
Hundreds of thousands of doctors practicing

00:11:27.690 --> 00:11:32.860
alone, thousands of hospitals, they don&amp;#39;t talk to each other.

00:11:32.860 --> 00:11:35.360
They each do their own thing.

00:11:35.360 --> 00:11:40.830
They&amp;#39;re all well meaning and most of them are phenomenally brilliant people,

00:11:40.830 --> 00:11:44.790
but that&amp;#39;s no way to run medicine.

00:11:44.790 --> 00:11:52.040
So increasingly, there&amp;#39;s a movement to integrate the delivery of care

00:11:52.040 --> 00:11:54.070
in the United States.

00:11:54.070 --> 00:12:00.900
So health care systems all over the world are terribly fragmented.

00:12:00.900 --> 00:12:07.440
In the US, there are over 500,000 doctors who for a long time

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:13.970
have pretty much practiced medicine as they see fit to practice medicine.

00:12:13.970 --> 00:12:17.430
Thousands of hospitals that have done the same.

00:12:17.430 --> 00:12:22.650
Clinics, nursing homes, all very fragmented.

00:12:22.650 --> 00:12:28.520
Some people who&amp;#39;ve studied the outcomes of this health care system

00:12:28.520 --> 00:12:33.410
say that this fragmentation is a real problem.

00:12:33.410 --> 00:12:37.790
Because due to the lack of standardization

00:12:37.790 --> 00:12:43.100
in the practice of medicine among all these thousands

00:12:43.100 --> 00:12:49.000
and thousands of doctors, hospitals, clinics, nursing homes,

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:54.140
there is not one way of doing things.

00:12:54.140 --> 00:12:58.750
Because there is not one way of doing things,

00:12:58.750 --> 00:13:03.660
people make mistakes that might be avoided if they

00:13:03.660 --> 00:13:07.560
adhere to more standardized protocols.

00:13:07.560 --> 00:13:11.210
When they make mistakes, two things happen.

00:13:11.210 --> 00:13:15.660
Quality goes down, costs go up.

00:13:15.660 --> 00:13:20.330
So in the US and in other developed countries,

00:13:20.330 --> 00:13:27.450
there is a movement to integrate these providers, to have systems of care

00:13:27.450 --> 00:13:36.360
rather than one of, two of, five of doctors or hospitals providing care.

00:13:36.360 --> 00:13:42.700
One form of this integration in the US is called the Accountable Care

00:13:42.700 --> 00:13:44.910
Organizations.

00:13:44.910 --> 00:13:52.050
These are systems and they consist of hospitals and doctors and nursing homes

00:13:52.050 --> 00:13:55.650
and clinics and urgent care centers.

00:13:55.650 --> 00:14:01.620
Essentially everything that you need if you need medical care.

00:14:01.620 --> 00:14:06.290
And they provide it out of one system that&amp;#39;s networked

00:14:06.290 --> 00:14:09.610
and talks to each other and all the providers

00:14:09.610 --> 00:14:14.120
know what everybody else is doing.

00:14:14.120 --> 00:14:17.700
The Accountable Care Organizations, they&amp;#39;re

00:14:17.700 --> 00:14:23.350
called ACOs with their initials, they might

00:14:23.350 --> 00:14:26.390
be very interested in this program.

00:14:26.390 --> 00:14:31.630
After all, this program searches through the medical literature

00:14:31.630 --> 00:14:36.260
and it comes up with what the medical literature has

00:14:36.260 --> 00:14:43.800
found to be the best answer to a specific kind of problem.

00:14:43.800 --> 00:14:47.900
In fact, I think they&amp;#39;d be real interested in that.

00:14:47.900 --> 00:14:52.090
And real interested for their providers.

00:14:52.090 --> 00:14:55.310
So that&amp;#39;s medical speak for their doctors,

00:14:55.310 --> 00:14:59.060
their nurses, their therapists, all the great people who

00:14:59.060 --> 00:15:01.780
are in the health care system.

00:15:01.780 --> 00:15:06.550
And they might be even be interested for their patients.

00:15:06.550 --> 00:15:09.580
So the patient i talked about earlier who

00:15:09.580 --> 00:15:16.370
says you want to put a pacemaker in me, I want to know how this works,

00:15:16.370 --> 00:15:20.950
how it works in the long term, what the positive side is,

00:15:20.950 --> 00:15:23.010
what the negative side is.

00:15:23.010 --> 00:15:23.890
OK.

00:15:23.890 --> 00:15:27.730
So we&amp;#39;ve got the ACO as a customer.

00:15:27.730 --> 00:15:38.110
The ACO is an integrator and this medical program enables integration.

00:15:38.110 --> 00:15:46.820
So what kind of person do we want to head this kind of new venture?

00:15:46.820 --> 00:15:48.420
Hold that thought.

00:15:48.420 --> 00:15:52.880
We&amp;#39;ll come back to this question in a little while.

00:15:52.880 --> 00:15:56.460
But before we even address this question,

00:15:56.460 --> 00:16:02.890
we want to figure out whether this thing is viable or not.

